C. THE ARC: what the Cold-Read team's work adds to the plan (proposals for the revision; the plan stays signed)

Their critic, journalist and five synthetic readers, read against the plan. Most of what they found the plan already carries (the hold, the locks, the freeze behind C031, the register as a record and not a gate, analysis over the night's logs). Six things it does not, each needing a register entry first where it has a number:
11. Chapter 1: the talk's own opening line said no hold had been pulled, and the script still said so after the 18:44 hold; the counter counted hold files present, not holds pulled. The strongest single scene in their work. Register, then set it beside the hold.
12. Chapter 8: "the five steps held" overstates it; near the deadline some wakes worked before they planned, two never closed, post-checks went partial (C022, C023). And the live-demo slides embedded the demo sites although security's conditions of 17:15 ruled embedding out, passed by a review that checked only for keys; most of the promised recorded fallbacks never came. The clearest control bent under the deadline.
13. Chapter 9: lead with the one expectation that never fired: across every wake record no review said stop (C023), the reviewers had the member's own reach, and only a person's quota stopped the team.
14. Chapter 2: a second failure. REALITY.md, held up as where truth is kept, was out of date at release on the talk, the studio pieces and the keys. (Also my plan's own error: at 298be4e BRIEF-CORRECTIONS.md still said none yet, as 02:97 found; the plan is corrected today.)
15. Chapter 5: the incident split ("three of five not attacks" against the slide's own two of five) is the C031 pattern a second time, open on the talk's side and never settled. Register, then add.
16. Chapter 0, before chapter 3's policies and chapter 12's "go build": the editor's own interest in the projects the talk points to is disclosed only on the about page. Readers who are buyers stop there. One plain sentence in his voice, early. And say once what each count counts (members staffed, merges, releases): their readers lost trust when the team's size and the talk's length were counted several ways.

D. THE EDITOR'S VOICE (the newsroom writer's rules)

The whole book is third person ("the editor", "the speaker"), so the revision is throughout, not chapter 6 alone. Where it will be hardest, or where he would not say it:
17. He would not praise himself: 00:40 "signed every decision that mattered"; 01:51 "the hold was right"; 03:95 his talk as "evidence"; 03:89 trim his chat quote praising his own approvals; 04:95 "a very good set of expectations"; 08:77 "the one person who could see"; 08:55 "the most important of the day"; 10:69, :79, :99, :117 his judgement as "a strong filter", his verdict "warm". State what he did; leave the verdict to the reader.
18. The book speaks for his motives and inner state: 06:21, :41, :49, :79, :85; 07:31; 04:75; 02:47; 03:17. In his voice each becomes his own account. RC1 is record only (O4), so where the record does not say, he should say "I do not know from the record" rather than the book guessing (10:61 is the clearest case).
19. He should own his own failures in the first person, which is where his voice is strongest: 11:39 (his live request that overrode a security condition), 10:63 (the $17.4M on his slide behind him), 12:103 and :11, :93, :133, :137.
20. Sources named and dated: 00:70 explorer, villager, town planner is his version of Simon Wardley's pioneers, settlers and town planners; credit it, with a date. 01:9 put the C021 caveat at the first mention of 95% ("I told the conductor we were at 95%").
